 ### Furniture
 
-A furniture surcharge is rent. Furniture and fitted kitchens are depreciated over their useful life (§ 7(1) EStG; lines 42 to 45). An item usable on its own costing not more than EUR 800 net may be deducted at once ([§ 6(2) EStG](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__6.html) through § 9(1) no. 7). How a furniture surcharge enters the cheap-let comparison is not on an official page read: refer.
+A furniture surcharge is rent. Furniture and fitted kitchens are depreciated over their useful life (§ 7(1) EStG; lines 42 to 45). An item usable on its own costing not more than EUR 800 net may be deducted at once ([§ 6(2) EStG](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__6.html) through § 9(1) no. 7). For the cheap-let comparison, use a furniture adjustment already provided by the local rent index without adding it twice. Otherwise add a surcharge only if it can be established from the local market for furnished lets; if no market value can be determined, no surcharge is added. Never derive it from monthly furniture AfA or a standard investment return ([BFH, 6 February 2018, IX R 14/17, paras. 18 to 20](https://www.bundesfinanzhof.de/de/entscheidung/entscheidungen-online/detail/pdf/STRE201810105?type=1646225765)).
 
